```
=== DIAGNOSIS ===
is_anomaly: true
faulty_devices: router2
diagnosis_text:
The FRR process group on router2 was externally killed — specifically watchfrr
(the supervisor, PID 39), zebra (PID 50), and bgpd (PID 57) were all
terminated. Only mgmtd and staticd remain alive. The stale PID files and
UNIX-domain sockets in /run/frr/ (all timestamped 08:14 at container startup)
confirm the daemons ran correctly at boot, established a BGP session with
router1, and exchanged the expected prefixes (200.1.1.0/24 outbound,
195.11.14.0/24 inbound), then were hard-killed with no logged shutdown event
(consistent with SIGKILL). Because watchfrr was killed alongside zebra and
bgpd, no supervisor remained to restart them.

The chain of failure: with bgpd dead, the BGP session to router1 dropped.
Router1's peer 193.10.11.2 entered the "Active" state, withdrew 200.1.1.0/24
from its RIB, and now returns ICMP "Destination Net Unreachable" for any
traffic toward pc2. In the reverse direction router2 still has the BGP-learned
195.11.14.0/24 route lingering in the kernel (not yet aged out), but that route
is orphaned — bgpd can no longer maintain it and it will eventually disappear.
The vtysh errors reported by NOC are a direct consequence of zebra being dead:
vtysh connects to the zebra.vty UNIX socket, which is now stale and has no
listener, causing every vtysh invocation to fail with a connection error.
pc2 can still reach router2 locally (200.1.1.1 on eth1) because that interface
is kernel-configured and unaffected by the FRR process state.

Root cause: watchfrr, zebra, and bgpd on router2 were all killed (injected
fault), removing the BGP session and all routing supervision on that device.

Corrective fix: restart FRR on router2 — run `service frr restart` (or
`/etc/init.d/frr restart`). This will respawn watchfrr, which in turn starts
zebra and bgpd, re-establishes the eBGP session with router1 within one
hold-timer interval (~90 s), and restores bidirectional reachability between
pc1 and pc2.
=== END ===
